(EXP-94: the add-0.5 rounding idiom on the largest literal the leaf can
 draw, a 13-digit whole part and two fraction digits, 15 significant
 digits in all. Each lands on the side of the .50 boundary the leaf
 predicts. The last pair is the contrast: 255269670110833.49 has 17
 significant digits, float64 stores it as 255269670110833.5, and adding
 0.5 and casting gives 255269670110834. That is float64's precision, not
 a compiler fault, and it is why the leaf caps the whole part at 13.
 Ran: vox EXP-94-precision.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   9999999999999
   9999999999999
   10000000000000
   8796093022207
   8796093022208
   255269670110833
   255269670110834
)
Print {9999999999998.99 add 0.5} as a number.
Print {9999999999999.49 add 0.5} as a number.
Print {9999999999999.50 add 0.5} as a number.
Print {8796093022207.49 add 0.5} as a number.
Print {8796093022207.50 add 0.5} as a number.
Print 255269670110833.49 as a number.
Print {255269670110833.49 add 0.5} as a number.
